1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,612
Book Four, Proposition Fifteen.

2
00:00:02,612 --> 00:00:07,490
In a given circle to inscribe an equilateral and equiangular hexagon.

3
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:16,610
Let A B C D E F be the given circle; it is required to inscribe an equilateral and equiangular hexagon in the circle A B C D E F.

4
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:27,058
Draw the diameter A D of the circle, take the center G, and with center D and radius D G describe the circle E G C H.

5
00:00:27,058 --> 00:00:35,800
Join E G and C G, carry them through to the points B and F, and join A B, B C, C D, D E, E F, and F A.

6
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:45,867
Because G is the center of circle A B C D E F and D is the center of circle E G C H, the segments G E, G D, and D E are all equal.

7
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:58,112
Thus triangle E G D—and similarly triangle D G C—is equilateral, so by One Five and One Thirty-Two the angles E G D and D G C are each one-third of two right angles.

8
00:00:58,112 --> 00:01:17,851
Since C G standing on E B makes adjacent angles equal to two right angles, the remaining angle C G B is also one-third of two right angles, and by One Fifteen the vertical angles B G A, A G F, and F G E are equal to them; hence all six central angles at G are equal.

9
00:01:17,851 --> 00:01:33,138
By Three Twenty-Six, equal central angles stand on equal arcs A B, B C, C D, D E, E F, and F A; and by Three Twenty-Nine, equal arcs are subtended by equal chords, so the hexagon A B C D E F is equilateral.

10
00:01:33,138 --> 00:01:47,757
Finally, adding arc A B C D to the equal arcs F A and D E makes arc F A B C D equal to arc E D C B A, so by Three Twenty-Seven angle F E D equals angle A F E, and all six interior angles are equal.

11
00:01:47,757 --> 00:01:57,182
Therefore the equilateral and equiangular hexagon A B C D E F has been inscribed in the circle, and its side equals the radius.

12
00:01:57,182 --> 00:01:58,740
Which was to be done.

13
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:16,554
Proposition Four Fifteen contains the celebrated Porism—cited by Proclus as the quintessential porism to a problem—that the side of the regular hexagon equals the radius of its circumscribed circle, dividing the circle into six equilateral triangles via a Vesica Piscis on radius G D.

14
00:02:16,554 --> 00:02:35,480
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixty-seven synthesizes twenty direct prerequisites across sixty-eight ancestor nodes, and powers the Book Thirteen golden-section theorems of Thirteen Nine and Thirteen Ten, where the squares on the hexagon and decagon sides sum to the square on the pentagon side.

15
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:49,642
